<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<!-- vita02a_meshcoll_realankle: vita02a_meshcoll.urdf with the REAL
     parallel-ankle pitch travel.

     The vita02a ankle is a parallel two-motor mechanism. The serial
     ankle_pitch_joint and ankle_roll_joint used here are virtual joints,
     while [-0.959931, 0.610865] rad is the travel of each physical motor.
     At roll=0 the virtual pitch can approach that motor travel, but pitch
     and roll share the same two motor limits, so the complete feasible set
     is coupled rather than a rectangle.

     This variant uses the SDK-aligned conservative virtual-joint AABB:
     pitch [-0.751, 0.414] rad and roll +/-0.436332 rad. Every point in that
     rectangle is intended to map within both motor limits. The pitch range
     is therefore a safe interval when the full roll interval is allowed;
     it is not the total pitch reachable at every fixed roll. Everything
     else - kinematics, inertials, collision geometry, dynamics - matches
     vita02a_meshcoll.urdf exactly.

vita02a_meshcoll: pure mesh-collision variant of vita02a.urdf.

     Identical kinematics, inertials, joint limits and dynamics; the only
     difference is the collision geometry. Exposed links collide with their
     own visual STL instead of the hand-tuned primitive proxies in
     vita02a.urdf; the feet use contact spheres (see below).

     Following Unitree's G1 convention, collision is NOT put on every link.
     A link fully enclosed by its neighbours carries no <collision> at all:
     any contact it could make is already reported by the link that swallows
     it, so the geometry only costs collision-check pairs. Measured enclosure
     in the default pose (fraction of a link's visual vertices inside the
     union of the other links' convex hulls) picked these:

       left/right_ankle_pitch_link     100.0%  inside knee AND ankle_roll
       neck_yaw_link                    84.6%  inside neck_pitch (head)
       waist_yaw_link                   82.8%  inside waist_pitch (torso)
       left/right_shoulder_pitch_link   76/66%  inside shoulder_roll

     Three further links are dropped by explicit choice, NOT because they are
     enclosed - they measure as exposed (left/right_shoulder_roll_link 2/0%,
     base_link 11.5%). Dropping them removes real contact geometry: the upper
     arms and the pelvis do not collide at all in this model. That is the
     intended trade for a locomotion-oriented model where the feet and legs
     carry ground contact, but if you need arm-vs-torso self-collision or a
     pelvis-first fall to register, restore a primitive on those links (see
     the tuned pelvis cylinder in vita02a.xml).

     The feet are the one place a mesh hull is actively wrong: each
     ankle_roll_link carries 10 contact spheres (r=0.005) tracing the sole
     outline, taken from urdf/vita02a_sphere_foot.urdf. Point contacts at the
     sole perimeter are the standard humanoid-locomotion setup and give a
     crisp support polygon, where a hull of the shoe mesh would round off the
     sole edges. (Unitree's G1 does the same with 4 corner spheres.)

     Use this where contact fidelity matters: self-collision checking, IK /
     motion-planning clearance (MoveIt, curobo), whole-body contact and
     retargeting. Mesh collision is markedly more expensive than primitives,
     so prefer vita02a.urdf for fast dynamics rollouts.

     Note most consumers (MuJoCo, Bullet, Drake) collide a mesh as its CONVEX
     HULL, so concave links read as fatter than the true shape. Consumers that
     honour concave collision meshes (e.g. trimesh/FCL-based checkers) use the
     exact STL. For true concave contact, run a convex decomposition instead.

     For the MJCF equivalent - which keeps tuned primitives on the feet and
     hands for stable ground contact - see ../vita02a_meshcoll.xml. -->
<robot name="vita02a_meshcoll_realankle">
  <link name="base_link">
    <inertial>
      <origin xyz="0.00218083 0.00030202 -0.00612254" rpy="0 0 0" />
